Issue: The memory a draw answers from is one file, and the retrieval already hands that file over, but the prompt sends the model looking for more of it on disk — and on the free-text task it spends most of the question's model-call budget doing so. `/harness` holds `solver/` and `memory/memory.json` and nothing else (benchmark/runtime/sandbox.py `harness_stage`), and `retrieve_examples` serves that memory where it fits: 8,665 characters for all 50 USPTO examples, 34,178 for all 200 of Symptom2Disease, 74,498 for 135 of LawBench's 200, measured over the frozen memories of run 4b32c77. Yet the USPTO samples of that run — five draws each — made 13.2 tool calls apiece against the draws' 5.08 retrievals: 271 `read_file`, 91 `glob`, 65 `ls`, 25 `grep` and 18 `execute` beyond them, 8.1 further calls per sample, each of which is a model call out of the question's 32 (18.8 calls per five-draw sample; ~1.6 of the 3.76 calls a draw spends). The reads are of the memory the retrieval already returned: `read_file` on `/harness/memory/memory.json` yields the raw 92,091-byte JSON — every example's full `input` string rather than the question/answer pairs the retrieval writes — and the model pages it 100 lines at a time, 26 reads over the five draws of conversations/0004/sample-1.json, while the globs look for files no memory has (`**/*oxidation*`, `**/*heterocycle*`). What the search adds is context rather than information: USPTO samples carried 209,639 characters of tool output on average, against the 5 × 8,665 = 43,325 the retrieval accounts for. That is the budget the draws themselves are taken from: at 3.76 calls per draw the parent's eight draws need ~30 of the 32 calls a question is given, and the parent's own five-draw run already spent 30 on one sample. Symptom2Disease explores little (1.1 non-retrieval calls per sample) and LawBench not at all (0.0; its 5.06 tool calls per sample are one retrieval per draw), so the cost falls entirely on the free-text task, which is the task with the headroom.

